<DocLayout
  title="Permissions & safety"
  description="Loopback by default, approval gates, path confinement, and the audit trail."
>
  <p>
    Sarthi Studio is <strong>local-first by default</strong>. The local
    service binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code>, the workspace is path-confined,
    and privileged actions stop for approval. Nothing is uploaded anywhere
    except the request you send to your router.
  </p>

  <h2>The control surface</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Loopback only.</strong> The service binds to 127.0.0.1; every
      UI and API path is served by the local process. Workspace routes reject
      non-loopback hosts and foreign origins.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Approvals and permission rules.</strong> Writes and commands in
      agent runs are gated by permission rules. When a step needs approval,
      the run waits; you inspect the proposed action and allow or deny it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Path confinement.</strong> Workspace paths are canonicalised;
      <code>..</code> escapes, absolute paths outside the folder, and
      symlinks leaving it are rejected. Binary files and files over 1 MB are
      refused by the editor API.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Audit trail.</strong> Agent actions, approvals, and outcomes
      are recorded so you can review and restore what happened.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Emergency stop.</strong> A global emergency stop halts agent
      activity at any time.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Security model, plainly</h2>
  <p>
    The trust boundary is the folder you open and the approval rules you set.
    Agent commands run with your user privileges — they are not OS-sandboxed
    — so the approval gate is the boundary. Installers and macOS builds are
    unsigned; verify checksums before installing, and expect Gatekeeper or
    SmartScreen warnings on first launch.
  </p>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Installers and macOS builds are unsigned; verify SHA-256 checksums before installing.</li>
    <li>Agent commands run with your user privileges, not in an OS sandbox.</li>
    <li>Remote MCP servers are trusted by configuration, not by default.</li>
    <li>Provider keys are stored in the router, not in Studio.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
